 | the translator's arrays short first (`jitfew`: 0, 1; Iteration 713; JIT designs): engine/jit.c - jit_body's arrays (at, arg, nat, opl, tl) 128 operations long in its frame, and 1,024 (jit_body_big) only for a word with more, translated again from its start; their heads were a page each at every depth of the callees' translation (~24 KB a frame). On eight JIT designs: memory (the selection's workloads but apps) -5 to -16 pages (-11 to -23 %), apps -7 to -27 pages, the engine +224 to +544 bytes, the outputs alike, the time in the noise | results/drafts/early-computers-713.md |
 | the chain hash with one constant (`hashk`: 0, 1; Iteration 729; every CV8 design - results/drafts/demoscene-ioccc-727.md, after the demoscene's crinkler): a name's chain ((v * 18835) >> 8) & (chains - 1), v kernel.4's HASH (the length and the first two characters) - K searched on the held-out programs for the ten roles and 16-128 chains at once (results/drafts/hashk-table-728.py; K = 256 is today's hash); forth/cv8-hashk.4's HASH8 in every dump becomes HASH's body (tools/layout.py --hashk, which threads the image by it too), the engine's L_x_hash and (FIND) by -DHASHK (vm-lab.c's CHAIN; gen-msc.py's); off, HASH8 leaves the image - every design's bytes as before. Measured (729, results/drafts/hashk-gene-729.py): the lookup's walk shorter - the smallest's parse -17.5 % of its instructions on the prototype. In the self-hosting compiler since 731: G-HASHK in THASH, cv8-hashk.4 among a design's overlays where HASH is no op | results/drafts/demoscene-ioccc-727.md |
+| each chain's most looked-up names first (`chainorder`: 0, 1; Iteration 732; every CV8 design - results/drafts/demoscene-ioccc-727.md, chainorder-margin-731.txt): the names looked up at least 64 times in the held-out programs (lab/evolve/chainorder-use.txt, results/drafts/chainorder-use-732.py) at the front of their chains, most first, the rest newest first as before; a link to a word laid later is its own form, three bytes (tag F0-FF; tag 2's E0-FF), carved from the four-byte one (28 and 29 bits back now); backward links as before. forth/cv8-chainorder.4 and cv8t-chainorder.4 in every dump - LINK-LEN8 (the narrower reach) and NEXT-NFA88 (the forward form) swapped in by tools/layout.py --chainorder, dropped off; the engine's PREVNFA and bfind's bf_prev by -DCHAINORDER. Measured (results/drafts/chainorder-732.txt, the ten roles): the images +56-120 B; the corpus's instructions 0.987-1.031 of off (the smallest, its walk Forth, 0.987; five C walks a little better, five a little worse); parse (held out) 0.79-0.99; with hashk the smallest's corpus 0.903. Not yet in the self-hosting compiler (G-NOT-YET; its forward links fixed at three bytes, so patched at the end - built if a role takes the gene) | results/drafts/chainorder-732.txt |
 | the JIT's stack cache (`jitk`: 0, 2, 3; Iteration 259; JIT designs): the top K cells stay in fixed registers across the copied code - tools/gen-jitk.py writes a variant of 16 hot data stencils (literal, DUP DROP SWAP OVER, + and immediate +, = > <, AND XOR, @ ! C@, I) for each cache state 0..K and a canonicaliser per state; control, calls, loops, exits and branch targets run in state 1, so every join and call agrees. Static stack caching as Ertl & Gregg's (up to 58% in their interpreter) and CPython's JIT; on the VM's Intel core mixed: loop -9%, bubble -5%, corpus -4%, fib +16% at K=2, flat at K=3 - the laptop decides. +2.3 KB (K=2), +2.8 KB (K=3) on the fastest design's engine |
 | link-time optimisation (`lto`: 0, 1; Iteration 260; CV8 designs): the engine and its runtime compiled as one (-flto), so GCC inlines and drops across them; the JIT's stencils are compiled outside it - LTO changed their code and the JIT stopped translating (fib 2.5x, loop 3.9x slower). Engines -32 (smallest) to -160 bytes (fastest) on top of Iteration 257's runtime savings; speed the laptop's |
 | GCC's computed-goto unfactoring (`gotodup`: 0, 1, 2 - its default 8, 32, 128 instructions; Iteration 260; CV8 designs): `--param max-goto-duplication-insns`. GCC factors computed gotos early and copies the dispatch back into handlers whose block ends in no more than this many instructions; larger values copy more - interpreted engines +704 to +800 bytes at 32-128 (3456ac1e5e, 697f12fecc); JIT engines unchanged. A dispatch per handler predicts better on some cores, a shared one is smaller - the laptop's |
